  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nCURRENT OVERNIGHT - THURSDAY NIGHT:\n\nBanded light to moderate rain is occurring across central \nMinnesota into northeast Minnesota as of 1230 AM CST where a \nTROWAL and stronger synoptic forcing for lift are present, with\nscattered rain showers intermixed with areas of drizzle for \neast-central Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin. As we continue \nthrough the overnight hours into daytime Wednesday, expect this \ngeneral footprint of rainfall to not change too significantly as\na vertically stacked low pressure system stalls over east- \ncentral Minnesota/west-central Wisconsin. High-resolution model \nguidance (REFS) does indicate some reinvigoration to more \nmoderate rainfall rates daytime Wednesday in the region of \nstrongest synoptic support in the TROWAL for central Minnesota \nto northeast Minnesota, as well as some orographic enhancement \nto rainfall along the North Shore. \n\nThe low then translates into northwest Wisconsin late Wednesday\nand exits slowly to the east Wednesday night through Thursday as\nthe upper-level cut-off low opens up and merges with a trough\ndiving down from Hudson Bay. This will gradually end the\nrainfall from west to east across northeast Minnesota Wednesday\nnight, though lingering moisture should keep light rain\npersisting in northwest Wisconsin into Thursday, particularly\nportions of the South Shore Thursday into Thursday night as\ncooler 850 mb temperatures slide over a relatively warmer Lake\nSuperior to produce light lake-effect precipitation before a\ndrier airmass wins out into Friday and cuts off precipitation. \nDepending on how much surface temperatures can cool Thursday\nevening and night in conjunction with lingering lake-effect\nprecipitation, some light snow could mix in for parts of the \nSouth Shore, namely the higher terrain of Ashland and Iron \nCounties where minor snowfall accumulations of a couple tenths \nof an inch could occur on elevated surfaces.\n\nAs for additional rainfall amounts with this system, the\nforecast ranges from 0.5-1.75\", with the lowest amounts in\neast-central Minnesota to inland northwest Wisconsin, and\nhighest amounts in the Arrowhead, far northern Minnesota, and\nportions of the South Shore. The bulk of this additional precipitation\noccurs daytime Wednesday into Wednesday evening.\n\nOne other thing to watch for will be strong northerly winds with\ngusts up to 40 mph possible along portions of the higher terrain\nin Cook and Lake counties Wednesday night into Thursday morning.\n\nFRIDAY - THIS WEEKEND:\n\nNo significant changes from the previous forecast for this\ntimeframe. Cooler and mainly drier air settles in for late this\nweek into weekend, with highs largely in the 40s and lows below\nfreezing, even dipping into the teens in some locations \nThursday night and Friday night. A slight warm up will then \narrive to the area late this weekend into early next week. An \nupper level shortwave grazing northern portions of the area\ncombined with a mostly dry cold front move through from Saturday\nevening into Sunday, bringing spotty and very low (10-20%)\nprecipitation chances to parts of the Arrowhead, Borderlands,\nand northwest Wisconsin. This would mainly be in the form of\nsprinkles or isolated to widely scattered rain showers if any\nprecipitation is realized.",
